WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.480
- This creepy crawler
sports a fiery red pigment

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.700
throughout its body,

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.040
and it's got a temperament to match.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.873
(whimsical music)

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.680
The Fireleg tarantula's body
is a brownish black color,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.350
with a rich red hue along it's legs.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.470
This spider is a mid-size
new world tarentula,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.050
growing around 5 to 6 inches.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.710
This particular species is easily provoked

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.740
when surrounded by other animals.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.830
When feeling threatened, they flick off

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.000
what are know as urticating hairs,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.270
which cause irritation

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.860
and a burning sensation to predators.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.190
Native to Mexico, the Fireleg tarentula

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.020
prefers dry scrub land.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.640
They are a terrestrial species often found

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.940
in burrows self made or
abandoned by rodents and lizards.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.610
The illegal pet trade, combined

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.410
with ongoing destruction
of natural habitat,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.210
causes considerable concern
for the future of this species.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.054
This is the Mexican Fireleg tarentula.

00:00:59.054 --> 00:01:01.887
(whimsical music)

